m after entering as visitorsappeared first onVanguard News.", "published_at": "2026-08-25 10:37:15+00", "image_url": null, "source": {"name": "Vanguard Nigeria", "tier": "international", "url": "https://www.vanguardngr.com"}, "bias_scores": [{"political_lean": 50, "sensationalism": 10, "opinion_fact": 25, "factual_rigor": 50, "framing": 10, "confidence": 0.15, "rationale": "\"{\\\"framing\\\": {\\\"connotation_score\\\": 7.8, \\\"keyword_emphasis_score\\\": 0.0, \\\"omission_score\\\": 38.3, \\\"headline_body_divergence\\\": 11.2, \\\"has_cluster_context\\\": true}}\""}]}}, {"article": {"id": "7becaec7-3923-454b-862e-49c3af7f0f47", "title": "Trump administration to revoke up to 200,000 visas of asylum seekers", "url": "https://www.axios.com/2026/08/24/trump-visas-asylum-seekers-revoke", "summary": "TheTrumpadministration plans to revoke up to 200,000 business and tourism visas held by people who've sought asylum in the U.S.,APfirst reported on Monday.Why it matters:It would be the largest massvisarevocation in U.S. history and would likely trigger legal challenges.What they're saying:\"We are coordinating with DHS to identify and revoke the nonimmigrant visas of foreigners who have come to the United States claiming to be short-term visitors, but then file for asylum to stay here permanently,\" State Department spokesman Tommy Pigott told AP.He wouldn't say how many visas might be revoked, adding, \"as the process will be ongoing, the number of revocations remains dynamic and will be done on a rolling basis.\"Representatives for the State Department and the U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services did not immediately respond to Axios' request for comment.The big picture:President Trump has madeimmigrationenforcement a key priority during his second administration,aggressively cra...", "published_at": "2026-08-24 21:12:46+00", "image_url": null, "source": {"name": "Axios", "tier": "us_major", "url": "https://www.axios.com"}, "bias_scores": [{"political_lean": 35, "sensationalism": 10, "opinion_fact": 25, "factual_rigor": 50, "framing": 18, "confidence": 0.288, "rationale": "\"{\\\"framing\\\": {\\\"connotation_score\\\": 17.5, \\\"keyword_emphasis_score\\\": 0.0, \\\"omission_score\\\": 36.0, \\\"headline_body_divergence\\\": 34.0, \\\"has_cluster_context\\\": true}}\""}]}}, {"article": {"id": "d2ae9615-8eac-4af1-bb92-0d7713c86ab0", "title": "US set for largest mass visa revocation in history targeting up to 200,000 foreigners: officials", "url": "https://www.greaterkashmir.com/world/us-set-for-largest-mass-visa-revocation-in-history-targeting-up-to-200000-foreigners-officials-12430002", "summary": "Washington, Aug 25:The Trump administration is preparing to revoke the business and tourism visas of up to 200,000 foreigners who have applied for or are currently seeking asylum status in the United States.If it happens, the move would be the largest single mass revocation of visas in US history and would likely face legal challenges.Unless challenged or revised, the State Department is expected to announce in the coming weeks the revocation of so-called B1 and B2 visas issued between 2016 and 2026 whose holders have sought asylum or are now seeking asylum, according to State Department documents obtained by The Associated Press and two US officials. The action will be taken in coordination with the Department of Homeland Security.“We are coordinating with DHS to identify and revoke the nonimmigrant visas of foreigners who have come to the United States claiming to be short-term visitors, but then file for asylum to stay here permanently,” said State Department spokesman Tommy Pigo...", "published_at": "2026-08-25 03:10:06+00", "image_url": "https://img-cdn.publive.online/fit-in/1200x675/greater-kashmir/media/media_files/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Trump-01-e1752116198405.png", "source": {"name": "Greater Kashmir", "tier": "independent", "url": "https://www.greaterkashmir.com"}, "bias_scores": [{"political_lean": 35, "sensationalis